      "text": "To find \\( f(3) \\) given the functional equation \\( f(x) + 2f(1 - x) = 3x^2 \\), we will substitute \\( x = 3 \\) and \\( x = 1 - 3 = -2 \\) into the equation and solve the resulting system of equations.\n\nFirst, substitute \\( x = 3 \\):\n\\[\nf(3) + 2f(1 - 3) = 3(3)^2\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\nf(3) + 2f(-2) = 27 \\quad \\text{(Equation 1)}\n\\]\n\nNext, substitute \\( x = -2 \\):\n\\[\nf(-2) + 2f(1 - (-2)) = 3(-2)^2\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\nf(-2) + 2f(3) = 12 \\quad \\text{(Equation 2)}\n\\]\n\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nf(3) + 2f(-2) = 27 \\\\\nf(-2) + 2f(3) = 12\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nWe can solve this system by elimination. First, label the equations for reference:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nf_1: f(3) + 2f(-2) = 27 \\\\\nf_2: f(-2) + 2f(3) = 12\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nMultiply Equation 2 by 2 to align the coefficients of \\( f(3) \\):\n\\[\n2f(-2) + 4f(3) = 24 \\quad \\text{(Equation 3)}\n\\]\n\nNow subtract Equation 1 from Equation 3:\n\\[\n(2f(-2) + 4f(3)) - (f(3) + 2f(-2)) = 24 - 27\n\\]\nSimplify the left-hand side:\n\\[\n2f(-2) + 4f(3) - f(3) - 2f(-2) = -3\n\\]\nThis reduces to:\n\\[\n3f(3) = -3\n\\]\nSolving for \\( f(3) \\):\n\\[\nf(3) = \\frac{-3}{3} = -1\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( f(3) \\) is \\(\\boxed{-1}\\).",
