      "text": "To solve for \\( f(3) \\), we start with the given functional equation:\n\\[ f(x) + 2f(1 - x) = 3x^2. \\]\n\nFirst, substitute \\( x = 3 \\) into the equation:\n\\[ f(3) + 2f(1 - 3) = 3(3)^2. \\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ f(3) + 2f(-2) = 27. \\]\nSo we have:\n\\[ f(3) + 2f(-2) = 27. \\tag{1} \\]\n\nNext, substitute \\( x = -2 \\) into the original equation:\n\\[ f(-2) + 2f(1 - (-2)) = 3(-2)^2. \\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ f(-2) + 2f(3) = 12. \\]\nSo we have:\n\\[ f(-2) + 2f(3) = 12. \\tag{2} \\]\n\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n1. \\( f(3) + 2f(-2) = 27 \\)\n2. \\( f(-2) + 2f(3) = 12 \\)\n\nWe can solve this system by elimination. First, let's express \\( f(-2) \\) from the second equation:\n\\[ f(-2) = 12 - 2f(3). \\]\n\nNow substitute this expression for \\( f(-2) \\) into the first equation:\n\\[ f(3) + 2(12 - 2f(3)) = 27. \\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ f(3) + 24 - 4f(3) = 27, \\]\n\\[ -3f(3) + 24 = 27, \\]\n\\[ -3f(3) = 3, \\]\n\\[ f(3) = -1. \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( f(3) \\) is \\(\\boxed{-1}\\).\n\nLet's verify the solution by substituting \\( f(3) = -1 \\) back into the original equations to ensure they are satisfied. If \\( f(3) = -1 \\), then from equation (2):\n\\[ f(-2) + 2(-1) = 12, \\]\n\\[ f(-2) - 2 = 12, \\]\n\\[ f(-2) = 14. \\]\n\nNow substitute \\( f(3) = -1 \\) and \\( f(-2) = 14 \\) into equation (1):\n\\[ -1 + 2(14) = 27, \\]\n\\[ -1 + 28 = 27, \\]\n\\[ 27 = 27. \\]\n\nThe solution satisfies both equations, confirming that the value of \\( f(3) \\) is indeed \\(\\boxed{-1}\\).",
